// Practice quiz: Python syntax for Java developers
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: python/python-fundamentals/python-syntax-for-java-developers

::o1 counting loop::A Java loop reads for (int i \= 0; i !\= 5; i++) and runs its body five times with i \= 0..4. Which Python line starts the same loop? {
~for (i \= 0; i !\= 5; i++)\: #Not quite. Python has no three-part for loop and no ++ operator; this is a SyntaxError.
=for i in range(5)\: #Correct. range(5) produces 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4, and the colon opens the indented body.
~for i in range(1, 5)\: #Not quite. range(1, 5) starts at 1 and stops before 5, so the body runs only four times.
~for i in 5\: #Not quite. An int is not iterable; Python raises TypeError. Wrap the count in range().
}

::o1 else if::You are translating a Java chain if ... else if ... else into Python. How is the middle branch written? {
=elif score \=\= 50\: #Correct. Python spells else if as one keyword, elif, followed by the condition and a colon.
~else if score \=\= 50\: #Not quite. else must be followed directly by a colon in Python, so this is a SyntaxError.
~\} else if (score \=\= 50) \{ #Not quite. Braces do not mark blocks in Python; indentation does.
~elseif score \=\= 50\: #Not quite. elseif is not a Python keyword, so the line is a SyntaxError.
}

::o2 floor division::What does print(-9 // 2) show in Python? {
~-4 #Not quite. That is what Java's int division gives, because Java truncates toward zero. Python's // floors.
~-4.5 #Not quite. That is the result of true division, -9 / 2.
=-5 #Correct. // is floor division\: -4.5 rounds down, toward negative infinity, to -5.
~-4.0 #Not quite. // on two ints returns an int, and the floor of -4.5 is -5, not -4.
}

::o2 true division::What does print(8 / 4) show in Python 3? {
~2 #Not quite. Only // returns an int for two ints; / always gives a float.
~2.00 #Not quite. Python prints the float 2.0 with one decimal place, not two.
~An error, because the result type was not declared #Not quite. Python names and expressions need no declared type.
=2.0 #Correct. / always returns a float, even when the division is exact.
}

::o2 truthiness::items \= [0]. Then\: if items\: print("has items") else\: print("empty"). What prints? {
~empty #Not quite. The list is not empty; the 0 inside it does not make the list itself false.
~0 #Not quite. The if tests the list; it does not print its contents.
=has items #Correct. A list is false only when it is empty. [0] holds one item, so it counts as true.
~TypeError #Not quite. Any list can be used as a condition; Python tests whether it is empty.
}

::o2 dynamic typing::x \= 10 then x \= "ten". What happens? {
~A TypeError at the second line #Not quite. Rebinding a name to a value of a different type is legal in Python.
~x becomes 10 converted to text #Not quite. Assignment does not convert; it simply points x at the new string.
~A compile error, as in Java #Not quite. Python does not check declared variable types before running.
=The name x now refers to the string; type(x) reports str #Correct. Names have no fixed type in Python; the value carries its type.
}

::o3 None test::Which Python condition is the usual way to write Java's if (user \=\= null)? {
~if user \=\= null\: #Not quite. null is not a Python name; this raises NameError.
=if user is None\: #Correct. None is a single object, so an identity test with is is the reliable way to check for it.
~if user.equals(None)\: #Not quite. Python objects have no equals() method; equality is written with \=\=.
~if user is null\: #Not quite. null is not a Python name; the no-value object is called None.
}

::o3 value or identity::Two separate lists hold the same values\: a \= [3, 4] and b \= [3, 4]. Which pair of results is right? {
=a \=\= b is True; a is b is False #Correct. \=\= compares values, like equals() in Java; is compares identity, like \=\= on Java references.
~a \=\= b is False; a is b is False #Not quite. \=\= compares the values, and the values are equal.
~a \=\= b is True; a is b is True #Not quite. is is True only when both names point to the same object, as after b \= a.
~Both raise TypeError #Not quite. Lists support both comparisons.
}
